      searchToolRequest: "Request",
      searchToolResponse: "Response",
      promptDepthEnhanced: "When reading notes, you may use read to read entire notes for complete information; keep the number of notes read under control.",
      promptContinueRound: `Above are this round's tool results. Re-assess coverage BEFORE answering: every region your analysis or earlier steps identified (a located table, a line range, a source to verify) must by now be actually READ. If ANY gap remains \u2014 a table located but not yet read, a segment not yet enumerated, a source not yet opened \u2014 calling tools to close it in THIS round is MANDATORY. An answer that announces, apologizes for, or works around a known gap ("I could not read the table", "the complete list would require reading X") is a FAILED answer: the user needs the content itself, not reading instructions. Only when nothing relevant is left unread, give the final answer directly.`,
      promptGapBounce: "Your draft answer above declares an unread gap \u2014 a table, section or source it located but never actually read (or rows it did not enumerate). REJECTED. Do not rewrite the draft from the same evidence. Instead: call read NOW on the exact region the draft named (file path + offset/limit; for a table, read ALL its segments and enumerate every row's values), then give the final answer containing the full enumeration.",
      gapBounceNote: "The draft answer declared an unread gap and was rejected once \u2014 the model is required to read the region it named before answering.",
      promptDepthStandard: "Prefer read to read only the relevant sections; avoid read on long notes to control context usage. When you hit a long table/matrix/checklist, table discipline takes precedence: read it fully in segments and enumerate every row before answering.",
      promptGrepGuide: "For questions like 'list/find all notes mentioning a keyword', a single grep call is enough: totalFiles is the total match count and paths holds the complete file list (when completeList is true). List them directly; do not call the tool again or re-search with a larger limit or different wording.",
      promptPrimarySource: "Primary source discipline: outline/extract/summary files are DERIVATIVES \u2014 they never substitute the original document. Before concluding on what a source document says (a clause, appendix, or requirement), locate the ORIGINAL full document and read the relevant part; when a user names an appendix/chapter, the original file may be titled after the document series instead (e.g. '2022 modification notice' contains its appendices). If find with one keyword misses, retry with broader ones: the document series name, issuing body, or year (e.g. 'modification notice', 'technical rules'). Only if no original exists in the vault, answer from the derivative and say so explicitly.",
      promptToolGuide: "You gather all evidence yourself - tool results are this turn's only source of notes. Search results are truncated previews: for exhaustive or precision-critical questions, read the relevant notes to verify before answering. To expand a hit into its surrounding context (chunk neighbors), grep a distinctive phrase from its preview to get the line number, then read(path, offset = line - 20, limit = 60). A grep zero match is a formatting problem, not absence - OCR inserts spaces inside phrases ('\u9644\u5F5512' may be stored as '\u9644\u5F55 12'); retry shorter/broader or read the file region directly. Time-related questions ('most recent', 'when was it recorded', 'last modified') use ls (sorted by modification time, take the first few); vault overview or listing notes uses ls; exact keywords, codes, numbers, dates use grep; meaning-based lookup uses search (mixed keyword+vector).",
      promptTableDiscipline: "Long tables / matrices / checklists (standard-clause tables, comparison tables) demand mechanical discipline: 1) grep first to build a line-number map, then re-read via read offset/limit in segments of no more than ~10,000 characters (a short table fits in one read) - never answer from one large read; 2) enumerate per segment: after each segment, list EVERY row of that segment (clause number + A/B/C value) before moving on; 3) coverage bookkeeping: before answering, state the covered line ranges; if the table end has not been read you MUST keep reading - never finish with 'only verified up to X'; 4) when several tables share the same clause-number shapes (e.g. Table 1 vs Table 2 in one file), declare each table's start/end lines first and enumerate them separately - never mix rows across tables. Two pitfalls: (a) read silently truncates a single OVERLONG line - this ONLY affects tables stored one row per physical line, where a whole <tr> sits on one line of thousands of characters (those need whole-line regex grep plus matchCount instead); HTML tables laid out one tag per line (<tr>, <td> each on its own short line) are normal readable text - segment-read them like any long table, and when unsure about a table's shape, probe with one small read (e.g. 20 lines) before deciding; (b) plain-text numbered headings (e.g. \u201C2.2.1 \u2026\u201D) never appear in the outline - prefer grep to locate them.",
      promptVectorAvailable: "Vector search status: AVAILABLE \u2014 the semantic tool works, and search fuses BM25 + vector results.",
      promptVectorUnavailable: "Vector search status: UNAVAILABLE (no embedding model configured) \u2014 never call semantic; search runs keyword-only (BM25). Use search / keyword / grep for retrieval.",
      promptNoFabricate: "Never fabricate information that does not exist in the notes. Start the final answer with the substance itself \u2014 never open with process narration like \u201CI have gathered enough information\u201D or \u201CNow here is the complete introduction\u201D; that narration belongs to the thinking phase, not the answer.Never use `---` horizontal rules to separate sections in the answer \u2014 connect sections with headings instead.",
      promptVersionPrecedence: "Version precedence: when retrieval returns multiple versions of the same document (e.g. V1.0.0, V2.0.0, V10.0), treat the highest-numbered version as authoritative \u2014 on conflicting content cite the newest and do not rely on superseded older versions. Exception: if an older version contains content the newer one lacks (removed clauses, appendices, details), that part remains valid \u2014 cite the older version for it and state which version the content comes from.",
